What I Learned About CNNs From My Chess Analyzer

When I started building my chess analyzer, I thought the hard part would be the chess rules. It wasn’t. 
The hard part was figuring out how to show a chessboard to a neural network, and why a CNN works better 
for that than a regular fully connected network.

Why a CNN?

A chessboard is an 8×8 grid, so it works a lot like a tiny image. CNNs are designed for data like this,
where the position of things matters. A knight on f3 means something different with pawns next to it than when it’s alone. 
A CNN looks at small regions of the board at a time, so it can pick up on those local patterns.

Another advantage is that the same filter is applied across the whole board, so the network doesn’t have 
to relearn the same pattern for every square. This also means fewer weights to train, 
which makes overfitting less likely.

How I Put the Board Into the Model

This was the biggest lesson for me. A neural network can’t read a FEN string, so I had to convert the board into numbers.
I used an 8×8×12 input, with one channel for each piece type and color. A 1 means that piece is on that square, and a 0 means it isn’t.

I used to think “channels” only meant colors, like red, green, and blue in a picture. 
Now I understand that a channel can be any information you want the model to see. You could add one for whose turn it is,
or for castling rights.

What Each Part Does
Convolution layers: find patterns in the position. Early layers detect simple features, and deeper layers detect bigger ideas.
Padding: matters a lot on a small board. Without it, the edges and corners get lost quickly.
ReLU: adds non-linearity, so the model can learn complex relationships instead of only straight-line ones.
Pooling: didn’t help much here. In chess, the exact square matters, so shrinking the board can throw away useful information.
Final layers: turn all the features into a single score for the position.
Problems I Ran Into
Imbalanced data. Most positions were close to equal, so the model became biased toward them.
Overfitting. The model did well on training data but poorly on new positions. Using a validation set and more data helped.
Tactics. A CNN is good at recognizing patterns but bad at calculating ahead. A tactic that is three moves deep is hard for it to see.
Choosing what to predict. Predicting the engine score, the game result, or the next move each teaches the model something different, so this choice matters.
Main Takeaways
Good data and a good input format matter more than a bigger model.
A CNN can’t replace search. Real chess engines combine a neural network with search. My CNN is more like intuition, and search is the calculation.
High accuracy isn’t enough. I have to test on positions the model has never seen and compare it against an engine like Stockfish.
Building it taught me more than reading about it. I understood convolutions and filters much better after debugging my own model.
Final Thoughts

My chess analyzer showed me that machine learning isn’t just about picking a model. It’s about understanding the problem, preparing the data well, 
and knowing what the model can and can’t do. If I keep working on this, I want to try combining the CNN with a search algorithm so it can handle tactics better.
